-- title:
--   A bounded nonempty polyhedron makes every dual system $A^{\mathsf T}y = c,\ y \ge 0$ solvable
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a real $m \times n$ matrix and $b \in \mathbb{R}^m$, and suppose the polyhedron
--
--   $$
--   P \;=\; \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : A x \le b\}
--   $$
--
--   is nonempty and bounded. Then for **every** $c \in \mathbb{R}^n$ there is $y \ge 0$ with
--   $A^{\mathsf T} y = c$.
--
--   That is: boundedness of a single polyhedron $\{Ax \le b\}$ forces the rows of $A$ to span all
--   of $\mathbb{R}^n$ with nonnegative coefficients -- the cone generated by the rows is the whole
--   space. The reason is that if some $c$ were not in that cone, Farkas would produce a direction
--   along which $c$ increases without leaving $P$, contradicting boundedness.
--
--   The statement is the hypothesis-discharging step of LP strong duality: once the primal is
--   known to be bounded and feasible, dual feasibility is automatic, and the two problems can be
--   compared. It is also what makes "bounded feasible region" a usable assumption in a
--   reformulation, since it hands over a dual-feasible point for any objective.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Boundedness is `Bornology.IsBounded`, so no norm has to be chosen; the
--   matrix-vector products are `Matrix.mulVec` and `Matrix.vecMul` written `*ᵥ`.
-- source:
--   Carried as an internal support lemma, with this exact statement and proof, in twelve accepted submissions across ten missions of the OR Formalization Drive (e.g. https://prove2.me/submissions/3c55ced4-4944-4de2-ba43-8110d2682db1); published here once so that it can be imported. A consequence of Farkas' lemma; see Schrijver, Theory of Linear and Integer Programming, §7.

import Mathlib

open Matrix

namespace LP

theorem exists_dual_of_isBounded {m n : Type*} [Fintype m] [Fintype n]
    (A : Matrix m n ℝ) (b : m → ℝ) (hne : {x | A *ᵥ x ≤ b}.Nonempty)
    (hbd : Bornology.IsBounded {x | A *ᵥ x ≤ b}) (c : n → ℝ) :
    ∃ y, 0 ≤ y ∧ Aᵀ *ᵥ y = c := by sorry

end LP
